ABSTRACT
This study examines the supercritical hydrocarbon fuel flows through additive manufacturing elliptical tubes using experimental and numerical method. The effects of ellipticity, AM roughness and operation conditions on the flow behavior of supercritical fluid and heat transfer are investigated. To evaluate the comprehensive thermal-dynamic performance of AM elliptical tubes, the heat transfer quantity per unit pressure drop is defined and performance evaluation creation is used. Experimental results show the heat transfer quantity per unit pressure drop of AM elliptical tube is more than twice that of the circular one, and the PECHET is 1.3 times higher than that of the elliptical tube with vertical major axis. The numerical results indicate that HET obtains a higher center turbulence intensity than VET. The fHET increases with the decreasing ellipticity and increasing surface roughness. fHET decreases about 0.0125 when the surface roughness is reduced by 5 μm. NuHET increases with the increasing surface roughness, and the heat transfer capacity increases by up to 108 % with an increase in roughness from 5 to 45 μm. Finally, the correlations of friction factor and Nusselt number for elliptical tube are concluded out, considered the AM roughness and ellipticity, based on the experimental and numerical data.
1. Introduction
Heat exchangers are unavoidable critical parts in the industrial processes involving heat transfer. They are used widely in chemical industries, oil refining, nuclear power, biochemical and gas turbine. Heat exchangers can be divided into heating and cooling according to the purpose of use. Active cooling is critical technology in new high speed aero engine. Both the cross section of cooling channel and the surface roughness make great effects on the pressure drop and heat exchanging efficiency. The booming metal additive manufacturing technology provides more new thinking for the enhance heat transfer technology[1–3], thermal management of high-flux devices, such as heat pipes[4,5] and compact heat exchanger[6,7] and discrete semiconductor packages [8,9]. In addition, the metal AM technology developing the optimized energy storage device related to multi-tube[10], hybrid PCM-air heat sink[11], lattice structures[12] ang triply periodic minimal surfaces [13]. For the increasing cooling requirements of more advanced aircraft, combining metal additive manufacturing and active cooling will become an effective method[14–18]. However, the inherent rough surface of
metal additive manufacturing products not only influences the mechanical strength[19–22], but also the flow and heat transfer performance [23,24]. Kaur and Singh [25,26] reviewed heat exchangers made by metal additive manufacturing and found that the inherent surface roughness is a common and key consideration in determining the thermal and hydraulic characteristics. Dai et al. [27] investigated the effects of surface roughness on liquid friction and transition characteristics in micro and mini-channels, revealing that both the friction factor and critical Reynolds number gradually deviate from the predicted values of smooth tubes. Forooghi et al. [28] investigated that the effect of roughness morphology and its scaling on the enhancement of heat transfer relative to skin friction. Peeters and Sandham [29] gained insight into the modification of turbulent heat transfer by an irregular rough surface, which has been shown to act as a surrogate for Nikuradsetype roughness. Huang et al. [30] investigated the effects of surface roughness on laminar flow in an organic glass tube and found that the critical Reynolds number decreases linearly with increasing relative roughness. Min et al. [31] demonstrated the potential of additive manufacturing in design and fabrication of transpiration cooling structures with high cooling efficiencies and the desired tensile strength. Yan et al[32] focused on the post-treatment of a Gyroid-structured heat
