4. Numerical setup
The numerical work is divided into two parts. The initial phase entails a comparison of the circular and elliptical cross-sections on the flow and heat transfer of supercritical hydrocarbon fuels. The related work is designed to elucidate the mechanism of circular and elliptical tubes on the flow behavior and enhance heat transfer in HET. The second part investigates the effects of surface roughness (Rz) on the flow and heat transfer of supercritical hydrocarbon fuel in AM elliptical tubes. The commercial soft fluent 2020R2 was used, the SIMPLEC scheme was
adopted and all absolute convergence criteria was set 10-5.
4.1. Computational grid
For ensuring grid reliability, accurate capture of both the velocity and thermal boundary layers is essential. An investigation into mesh independence is carried out for the HET(e = 0.515). A structured grid is generated for both elliptical and circular tubes, as depicted in Fig. 7, this grid is extended along the entire tube length. Fig. 8 shows the calculation and comparison of the friction factor and Nusselt number with those obtained from different grid systems. The friction factor and Nusselt number exhibits significant oscillations until Nm reaches 2.3 million, after which they only minor changes as Nm increases from 3 to 5.2 million. Therefore, the computational grids for HET consist of 2,974,140 hexahedral cells, which provide sufficient accuracy in simulating the flow and heat transfer characteristics. In this mesh, the distance between
the nearest node and the tube wall is 0.0002 mm with y+<1. The growth ratio was set at 1.05, and the number of rows in the boundary layer was set at 15.
