4.3. Model validation
The numerically predict results of the elliptical tube are compared with experimental results for Reynolds number in the range of 6000–15000, an ellipticity of 0.515, and a surface roughness (Rz) of 45 μm. The numerical simulation of the flow and heat transfer of the AM elliptical tube is performed using shear stress transport (SST) k-w, Standard k-w and Standard k-ε turbulence models to assess their suitability for calculating flow and heat transfer in elliptical tubes. The Rz represents the sum of the average of the five largest contour peak heights and five largest contour valley depths within the sampling length (0.8 mm). Due to the sampling length is so short that Rz is the height of pins, and these peak and valley can decide the flow condition near the wall. The roughness height of internal wall is consistent under the specific processing conditions. Therefore, the equivalent sand-grain roughness model was used, and the roughness height is Rz. To simulate the flow and heat transfer correctly, the roughness constant was tried and choose roughness constant of 0.6 is more appropriate. The performance parameters of the present study, including the friction factor and Nusselt number, are compared with the corresponding experimental data, as shown in Fig. 9(a) and (b). The numerical results obtained using the k-w SST turbulent model exhibit the highest level of agreement with the experimental data. Therefore, the k-w SST turbulent model was selected to simulate the flow and heat transfer characteristics in AM elliptical tubes.
5. Results and discussion
5.1. Comparison of flow and heat transfer between AM elliptical and circular tube
The pressure drop and friction factor for elliptical and circular tubes under various operational experimental conditions are shown in Fig. 10. The pressure drops of circular tube are higher than those of elliptical tubes, as the result of the higher velocity in the circular tube with the same hydraulic diameter, leading to stronger turbulence intensity of the supercritical hydrocarbon fuel compared to elliptical tubes. The difference in pressure drops between AM elliptical tubes with horizontal major axis (HET) and vertical major axis (VET) is small, indicating the direction of the major axis for elliptical tubes has little significant impact on the flow resistance. As shown in Fig. 10(a) and (b), the pressure drop and friction factor
of elliptical and circular tube both increase with increasing Re. Further investigation finds that the fluid velocity in elliptical tubes is lower than that in circular one. This is due to the larger cross-sectional area of elliptical tubes at the same hydraulic diameter. In contrast to the rate of pressure drop increase, the rate of friction factor increase for AM circular tubes is greater than that for elliptical tubes. Furthermore, the friction factor of the elliptical tubes initially increases to maximum values and then undergoes minimal changes as the Re increases. The pressure drops in elliptical and circular tubes increase with increasing heat flux, as shown in Fig. 10(c). This increase can primarily be attributed to the change in the thermophysical properties of the supercritical hydrocarbon fuel under high pressure[51]. The increase in heat flux raises the fuel temperature, leading to a decrease in fuel viscosity. Concurrently, the increased fuel temperature reduces the fuel density. Moreover, as the heat flux increases, the decreasing rate of friction factor for AM circular tubes surpasses that of elliptical tubes. As can be observed in Fig. 10(e), the pressure drop decreases with increasing system pressure. This can be attributed to the density increase, which consequently leads to reduced velocity. Additionally, the viscosity increases leads to higher shear stress on the wall, resulting in reduced velocity. Both of these factors contribute to the decrease in the pressure drop and friction factor with the increasing system pressure. Upon comparing the heat transfer coefficient(h) and Nusselt number (Nu) of AM tubes from Fig. 11, it is evident that the HET is more effective for heat transfer. As illustrated in Fig. 11(a) and (b), the results indicate that the heat transfer coefficient(h) of the three AM tubes increases with an increasing Re. This can be attributed to the reduction of the thermal boundary layer and the escalation of turbulent intensity with increasing Re. When Re blow 10000, the hVET is almost equivalent to hCT. However, when Re exceeds 10000, the hVET surpasses hCT, and the difference between them increases significantly. This is due to 10,000 being the critical Reynolds number for full turbulence, and the effect of Reynolds number on heat transfer efficiency in the AM elliptical tube becomes more significant above this threshold. Observations indicate that both the heat transfer coefficient (h) and the Nusselt number (Nu) increase with the escalating heat flux lightly, as delineated in Fig. 11 (c) and (d). This trend can be ascribed to the accelerated fuel velocity, leading to a thinner thermal boundary layer and improves the heat transfer efficiency overall. The h and Nu of three AM tubes exhibit a downward trend as the system pressure escalates, as depicted in Fig. 11 (e) and (f). This trend can be explained by the fact that an increase in system pressure leads to higher fuel density, resulting in decreased the fuel velocity. Concurrently, the amplified system pressure augments the thermal conductivity of fuel, thereby enhancing the heat transfer process. However, it is important to note that the impact of the decreasing flow velocity on heat transfer is more significant than the influence of the increasing thermal conductivity.
