5.2. The mechanism of heat transfer enhancement in AM HET
As illustrated in Fig. 12(a) and (c), owing to the influence of secondary flows within the cross section, the temperature adjacent to the side wall of HET is the highest. As depicted in Fig. 12 (d), the top temperature of HET is the lowest. This is because the thermal and velocity boundary layers exhibit considerably thicker along the sides of HET as shown in Fig. 12(a) and (b). While they are significantly thinner at the top and bottom. The interplay between wall curvature and secondary flow creates a more advantageous heat transfer boundary condition at
the top of HET. Conversely, the thermal and velocity boundary layers within VET exhibit a markedly contrasting thickness. The distributions of velocity and turbulent kinetic energy along the central axis of three AM tubes are depicted in Fig. 13. It is discernible that the turbulent kinetic energy is higher in circular tube than in elliptical tubes. The increased speed observed in circular tubes can be attributed to their reduced cross-sectional area in comparison to elliptical tubes. As evident from Fig. 13(c) and (d), both the axial center velocity and turbulent intensity of HET surpass those of VET. This difference is the principal factor contributing to the superior heat transfer
