5.3. Performance evaluation criterion for AM elliptical tube
Superior heat transfer performance is typically accompanied by a significant increase in pressure drop. To investigate how cross-sectional shapes impact the pressure drop and heat transfer of AM elliptical tubes with identical hydraulic diameters, the parameter ξ is introduced to represent heat transfer quantity per unit pressure drop.
ξ= Q
Δp (7)
Here, Q and Δ p represents the quantity of heat transfer and pressure drop, respectively. Additionally, to evaluate the comprehensive thermal–hydraulic performance of the AM HET and VET, the f and Nu values of an AM circular tube are used as reference condition. The performance evaluation criterion (PEC) for different structures at a constant flow rate can be calculated as follows:
PEC = (NuET/NuCT)
(fET/fCT) (8)
The comparison of the ξ values for three AM tubes within the experimental range is shown in Fig. 14(a). It is evident that the circular tube has the smallest ξ value, while the HET has the largest ξ value, and the VET falls in between. The ξ value of the HET is twice that of the circular tube and 1.5 times that of VET. These results indicate that the
HET possesses the highest heat transfer capacity within the given range of operating parameters. Fig. 14(b) illustrates that all PEC values for the elliptical tube are greater than 1, and the PEC value for the HET is 1.3 times that of the VET. Additionally, the PEC of the elliptical tube decreases with an increase in q. Furthermore, it considered that a system pressure of 3.5 MPa is optimal, while a Re value of 8000 is the least effective heat transfer in AM elliptical tubes. Which can be explained by Re of 8000–12000 is the critical point of turbulent flow, however, the great surface roughness and the secondary flow in the elliptical tube advance the critical point of complete turbulent flow to 8000. With the increasing Re, the heat transfer performance enhanced. When 2300 < Re < 8000, the flow is in the transition region between laminar flow and turbulent flow, and the flow heat transfer is unstable, the PEC decreases with the increasing Re. Therefore, the PEC of both HET and VET achieved a minimum value at the Re of 8000.
