5.5. Effects of surface roughness on flow and heat transfer in tubes
To investigate the effects of roughness on the flow and heat transfer in elliptical tube, the Rz was chosen as the roughness height and adjust the roughness constant was adjusted to 0.6, enabling the k-w SST turbulent model to accurately simulate flow and heat transfer in elliptical tubes. The flow characteristics and heat transfer of an elliptical tube with an ellipticity of 0.515 and the surface roughness of 5 μm, 15 μm, 25 μm, 35 μm and 45 μm are illustrated in Figs. 18-19, respectively, for Reynolds number ranging from 6500 to 15000. As shown in Fig. 18(a), the pressure drop across the tubes increases with both surface roughness and Reynolds number. This is attributed to the increased obstruction of fluid flow and subsequent rise in pressure drop as the surface roughness increases. Consequently, the friction factor also increases with the rising surface roughness, as shown in Fig. 18(b). It can be observed that reducing the surface roughness by 5 μm leads to a decrease in the friction factor of approximately 0.0125. However, as the Reynolds number increases, the disparity in pressure drop between HETs with a surface roughness difference of 5 μm also increases, reaching up to 0.5 KPa at a Reynolds number of 15000. Fig. 19(a) and (b) illustrate the coefficient of heat transfer and Nusselt number, respectively, for HETs with varying surface roughness. The coefficient of heat transfer is directly proportional to the surface roughness area. At the Reynold number of 15000, the HET with surface roughness of 45 μm exhibits the highest coefficient of heat transfer, with an enhancement of 108 % compared to the HET with 5 μm roughness. As depicted in Fig. 18(b), the Nusselt number increases with both increasing surface roughness and Reynolds number. When Reynolds number increases from 6500 to 15000, the difference in Nusselt number for HETs with the surface roughness values differing by 5 μm range from 25 to 50.
5.6. Friction factor and Nusselt number correlations
Due to the lack of correlations of flow and heat transfer in the elliptical tube, a well-known correlation for predicting Nusselt number of circular tubes proposed by Dittus-Boelter, is adopted in the verification. Unfortunately, this classic correlation has been determined that
cannot predict flow and heat transfer in AM HETs well, as shown in Fig. 20. Owing to the crucial of elliptical tubes in industry, and the empirical correlation is of great significance for the design of relevant equipment, new correlations of friction factor and Nusselt number need be developed. To predict the flow resistance of fuel in AM HET accurately, the correlation takes into account the effects of both ellipticities and surface roughness. The surface roughness needs dimensionless treatment, and the dimensionless roughness being the relative roughness(ε), which is defined as the ratio surface roughness to hydraulic diameter. Therefore, the dependent variable of the correlation is friction factor(f), and the independent variables are Re, e and ε. The general form of correlation is as follows:
