6. Conclusions
Experimental investigation was conducted to determine the supercritical thermodynamic of AM elliptical and circular tubes. The experimental results are analyzed to determine the effects of Re, heat flux, system pressure, ellipticity and direction of major axis of elliptical tube on the flow behavior and thermal performance. The heat transfer quantity per unit pressure drop is defined and PEC was used to evaluate the comprehensive heat transfer capacity. Numerical simulations are implemented to reveal the enhancement mechanisms of HET on the flow and heat transfer process. Moreover, the flow and heat transfer capacity of HETs with various AM surface roughness values was investigated through simulation and experimental validate. The conclusions of this study are presented below:
(1) The pressure drops and friction factor of the AM circular tube are 1.2 times greater than those of the AM elliptical tube. The ξHET twice as great as ξCT. The pressure drop of HET and VET with same ellipticity is nearly the same. However, the h and Nu values of AM HET are about 1.2 times greater than those of VET. The ξHET is 1.5 times greater than ξVET. The PECHET is 1.3 times greater than PECVET. (2) The ξ of AM elliptical tube increases with system pressure and heat flux, but decreases with increasing Re. Initially, the PEC increases with system pressure, but then decreases, with 3.5 MPa identified as the optimal system pressure. The PEC decreases initially with increasing Re, but then increases, with Re = 10000 identified as the optimal Re.
(3) Comparing with a circular tube, the low curvature wall of the HET is more conducive to the secondary flow from both sides of the wall. This results in a higher level of turbulence among the fluid particles within the section, which in turn enhances heat transfer. (4) The fHET increases as the ellipticity decreases. The annular flow regime in HET results in a greater heat transfer performance for HETs than for circular tubes with the same hydraulic diameter. Owing to the greater surface roughness with the bigger rough microstructure, which can destroy the flow and thermal boundary layer, the fHET and NuHET increase with the increasing roughness. (5) Base on numerical and experimental data, an empirical correlation between the friction factor and the Nusselt number for the HET have been proposed. Two correlations considered the effects of ellipticity, surface roughness and operating conditions on the flow and heat transfer comprehensively.
